- High-contrast mode.
- Colour-blind-safe indicators.
- Reduced motion.
- Haptic and sound controls.
- Left-handed keypad layout.
- One-handed calculator access.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Switch-access compatibility.
- Clear error messages.
- Accessible graphs with textual descriptions and traceable values.
- Accessible alternatives for drag-and-drop games.
- Adequate touch targets.
- Localisable strings and layouts.

Mathematical content must remain understandable when read aloud. Provide spoken descriptions or structured linear alternatives for complex expressions where supported.

Test with real accessibility services and manual keyboard and switch navigation, not only automated scanners.

---

14. UI STATE AND INTERACTION QUALITY

Use predictable, recoverable state handling.

Every asynchronous screen must support:

- Loading.
- Success.
- Empty state.
- Recoverable error.
- Offline state where relevant.

Do not use endless spinners.

Preserve expression edits when opening a help panel or switching explanation modes.

Confirm destructive operations such as deleting saved notes.

Never require an account merely to dismiss onboarding or access the calculator.

Avoid notifications by default. If optional reminders are added, explain their purpose and allow easy disablement.

---

15. TESTING AND VERIFICATION

15.1 Unit tests

Test:

- Arithmetic semantics.
- Expression parsing.
- Operator precedence.
- Exact arithmetic.
- Rounding.
- Trigonometric conventions.
- Equation solving.
- Unit conversions.
- Dimensional analysis.
- Numerical convergence.
- Matrix operations.
- Graph evaluation.
- Mastery updates.
- Revision scheduling.
- Content schema validation.

15.2 Integration tests

Verify:

- Calculator to derivation.
- Calculator to graph.
- Physics solver to units.
- Practice to mistake journal.
- Games to assessment.
- Simulation to experiment history.
- Guest mode to local persistence.
- Account sync and conflict recovery.
- Content updates and rollback.
- Account deletion and export.

15.3 UI tests

Test all primary navigation, essential interactions, empty states, errors, screen rotation, theme changes, font scaling and offline operation.

15.4 Property and regression tests

Use property-based tests for supported mathematical invariants.

Examples:

- Valid inverse operations recover the original value within the documented tolerance.
- Unit conversion round trips preserve equivalent quantities within tolerance.
- Matrix inversion is rejected for singular matrices.
- Supported graph evaluation agrees with the engine's evaluation at test points.
- Equivalent supported expressions produce equivalent results.

Maintain a regression test for every fixed defect.

15.5 Device testing

Test on:

- A low-memory Android device.
- A mainstream phone.
- A tablet or large-screen emulator.
- At least one supported older Android version.
- Current supported Android versions.

Do not claim universal compatibility from emulator tests alone.

15.6 Proposed release targets

These are hypotheses to measure, not achieved facts.

- No known critical calculation defects in release-blocking test suites.
- No known critical privacy or security defects.
- At least 99.5% crash-free sessions as an initial product-health target, measured with privacy-appropriate telemetry.
- Responsive basic calculation interaction under normal device load.
- Smooth graph interaction on the declared minimum supported device.
- No mandatory network dependency for core offline features.
- No blocking accessibility defects in primary flows.

Record actual measurements, test environments and remaining limitations.

---

16. PERFORMANCE AND RESOURCE BUDGETS

Set explicit budgets before optimisation.

Initial proposed goals:

- Cold startup: target approximately 2 seconds or less on a defined reference device.
- Basic calculation: target approximately 100 milliseconds or less under normal load.
- Lightweight screen transitions: avoid unnecessary main-thread work.
- Graph updates: maintain responsive interaction on the minimum supported device.
- Memory: avoid loading unnecessary game assets and large models into RAM.
- Initial installation: keep the essential application lean; distribute optional content separately where permitted.

These targets are engineering goals, not guarantees. Benchmark actual release builds on defined hardware.

Use profiling, Baseline Profiles where applicable, memory diagnostics and macrobenchmarks to guide improvements.

Do not load an on-device AI model on startup unless the student explicitly enters a feature that requires it.

